\section{What a set carries, and what it does not}{

User metadata is \strong{tags}, and there is no \verb{metadata =} parameter: a
description is a tag, and a second channel for the same thing would be two
places to look. There is no view or navigation configuration either -- a
folder-like hierarchy is a projection a consumer computes over tags, and any
number of them cost nothing precisely because none is stored.

A set records \strong{no} \code{parents} and \strong{no} \code{source_lineage}. Members are
references, not derivation: lineage flows through tables, and the set is how
you \emph{found} a table rather than how data reached it.
}

\section{Versions, and what moves one}{

The version covers the \strong{whole payload}, members and tags alike. So editing a
tag or a description mints a new version, which is intended: a set exists to be
citable, and "same citation, different labels" would be a lie to whoever cited
it. What does \strong{not} mint a version is a purely syntactic edit -- reordering
tag values or members, repeating a label, or writing a single label as a
one-element array. Those are normalised on the way in, so re-writing an
identical payload is a no-op.

\strong{Your code does not move a version either, even though it travels in the same
commit} (see \code{include_paths} below). Refactor your build script, re-run it,
get the same members and tags, and nothing is minted: the write is the usual
no-op. \code{\link[=datom_history]{datom_history()}} then shows the version it showed before, with a
\code{commit_sha} pointing at the commit that \strong{first} produced that payload -- a
commit that does not contain the code you just wrote. That is the recorded
value doing its job rather than going stale; see \code{\link[=datom_history]{datom_history()}} for why the
commit is deliberately not part of the version.
}

\section{Where the payload lives}{

Two copies, at two deliberately different addresses. Git holds
\code{{name}/set.json} at one stable path, modified in place, so git carries the
history and \verb{git diff} between two versions shows which members changed.
Storage holds the same bytes content-addressed at \verb{\{name\}/\{data_sha\}.json}, so
a reader with no clone can fetch an exact version. Any past version is still
reconstructible from the clone alone with
\verb{git show <commit>:\{name\}/set.json}.
}

\section{Carrying your code and environment into the same commit}{

\code{include_paths} stages paths you name into the \strong{one} commit that carries the
payload and its metadata. So checking out a set version's commit yields the
data pointers, the logic that produced them \strong{and} the environment they ran in
-- one clone, one checkout, the whole product. The joint version is
\strong{structural}: nothing records a link between the set and your files, because
the commit \emph{is} the link.

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{datom_write_set(conn, members,
                include_paths = c("R", "dp", "renv.lock"))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

